WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.981
Injection prevention should be visible in design, code review, and tests.

2
00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:11.939
Reviewers should ask where untrusted data enters, where it flows, and which sensitive sinks it reaches.

3
00:00:11.939 --> 00:00:21.980
Sensitive sinks include database queries, shell commands, templates, browser rendering, file paths, headers, URLs, parsers, and downstream APIs.

4
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:28.543
If data reaches one of those locations, the review should identify the context-specific control.

5
00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:35.896
Security-focused tests should cover schemas, negative cases, boundaries, and high-risk transformations.

6
00:00:35.896 --> 00:00:52.263
A test can confirm that unknown fields are rejected, that unsupported operators are not accepted, that route parameters are treated as values, that rendering paths use safe APIs, and that command execution paths only allow approved operations.

7
00:00:52.263 --> 00:00:58.509
These tests should use safe representative inputs rather than exploit payload collections.

8
00:00:58.509 --> 00:01:04.439
Static analysis and dependency review can help, but they are not complete by themselves.

9
00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:12.583
Tools may find string-built queries, risky process execution calls, raw HTML rendering, or unsafe evaluation patterns.

10
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:20.647
Human review is still needed to understand data flow, framework behavior, business intent, and compensating controls.

11
00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:24.126
Least privilege and defense in depth reduce impact.

12
00:01:24.126 --> 00:01:29.187
Database accounts should have only the permissions the application needs.

13
00:01:29.187 --> 00:01:32.665
Processes should run with constrained privileges.

14
00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:39.386
Logging and monitoring should identify suspicious validation failures without exposing secrets.

15
00:01:39.386 --> 00:01:49.427
Content Security Policy, safe defaults, and framework protections can add layers, but they should not be treated as permission to build unsafe sinks.

16
00:01:49.427 --> 00:01:52.353
Document assumptions and sensitive sinks.

17
00:01:52.353 --> 00:02:04.687
Future developers need to know why a field is allowlisted, why a query builder escape hatch is prohibited, why a template path rejects user content, or why a rendering helper is required.

18
00:02:04.687 --> 00:02:09.510
Secure design becomes easier to maintain when the reasoning is visible.
